    <h3 id="description">Description</h3>

<p>In 2015, to demonstrate the enormous size of the private keyspace (or perhaps just for fun), someone created a
    "puzzle" where he chose keys in a certain smaller space and sent increasing amounts to each of those keys like
    this:</p>
<p>
    2<sup>0</sup> &le; random key &lt; 2<sup>1</sup> &mdash; 0.001 BTC<br>
    2<sup>1</sup> &le; random key &lt; 2<sup>2</sup> &mdash; 0.002 BTC<br>
    2<sup>3</sup> &le; random key &lt; 2<sup>3</sup> &mdash; 0.003 BTC<br>
    ...<br>
    2<sup>255</sup> &le; random key &lt; 2<sup>256</sup> &mdash; 0.256 BTC<br>
    (total 32.896 BTC)</p>

<p>Original <a href="https://bitcointalk.org/index.php?topic=1306983.msg18765941#msg18765941">puzzle description</a>
    from the creator:</p>

<figure class="border rounded p-3">
    <blockquote class="blockquote fs-6">
        <p>"A few words about the puzzle. There is no pattern. It is just consecutive keys from a deterministic
    wallet (masked with leading 000...0001 to set difficulty). It is simply a crude measuring instrument, of
    the cracking strength of the community."</p>
    </blockquote>
            <figcaption class="blockquote-footer m-0">
            saatoshi_rising on BitcoinTalk
        </figcaption>
    </figure>

<p>First #1&mdash;70 and #75, #80, #85, #90, #95, #100, #105, #110, #115, #120, #125, #130, #135 private keys have already
    been found.</p>

